; 13-todo.kurt

; `todo` is a joker: it lets a proof continue -- and the file still "check
; out" -- even though a step has not actually been justified.  It is meant
; for iteratively developing a proof, or for handing out a proof skeleton
; with the hard parts left as exercises.

bool p, q

show p
proof
    todo p          ; admit `p`, with a note that this needs real work
qed

show p implies q
proof
    todo             ; admit whatever the block's goal is, no note needed
qed

; A file with unresolved `todo`s does not report "Proof checked" -- Kurt ends
; with "Proof almost checked: N todos." instead, and lists exactly where each
; one is, e.g. "13-todo.kurt:12 todo p".  That is Kurt's built-in reminder
; list: the file runs (nothing is broken), but it's not finished yet.
